          "type": "string"
        },
        "model_exceeded": {
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "fit_r2": {
          "type": "number"
        }
      },
      "description": " `fit_family` names whose model this is (the session's own sport family, fitted on the 90 days STRICTLY BEFORE it, so a session is never inside its own fit). A NEGATIVE `min_j` is the model failing, not depth achieved \u2014 it means more above-CP work was done than the fitted tank holds, so the fit is stale or degenerate; `model_exceeded` flags exactly that. Normalize with `min_j / w_prime_used` before comparing sessions, since `w_prime_used` moves between dates. When `model_exceeded` is true, ONLY `min_j` is meaningful: the recovery term drives on the deficit against the CURRENT balance, so a balance far below zero reconstitutes faster than the model's own ceiling allows and `end_j` is arithmetic on a broken premise. `fit_r2` is how well the CP line fit its own points (1 by construction at `fit_points` 2). CP jumps at window edges as rides enter and leave the 90 days, so treat these as WITHIN-session measures rather than a comparable series."
    }
